  "reasoning": [
    "Money.FromPiastres intentionally represents signed long facts; it does not assert a positive payment. Negative balances, net amounts and differences can be legitimate, so banning negative Money globally would regress financial meaning.",
    "Deposit explicitly requires AmountPiastres>0 before constructing movement; aggregate paid validation in PurchasingRules does not establish positive receipt quantity/unitcost. FIN018 remains a command/line admission defect, not evidence that every signed monetary field must be positive.",
    "Checkout accepts signed fee and attaches authorization to distinct positive discount/comp fields, established FIN016. Shared Money or client input helper does not close this server boundary.",
    "CashCount handler delegates denomination values to Domain; Quantity clamp is intentional but denomination itself and multiplication/difference remain existing cash-count finding. LINQ Sum has different overflow behavior from unchecked per-row multiplication; do not claim all arithmetic wraps."
  ],
  "counterevidence": [
    "Different commands have legitimate distinct sign/zero policies: external capital deposit positive, fully comped/zero delivery fee valid, discrepancies and balances signed.",
    "Existing Deposit gate is positive counterevidence to blanket missing financial amount validation; no new finding count, source functions not re-promoted."
  ],
  "recommendations": [
    "Define explicit typed/command-level amount and range invariants before constructing shared Money facts. Preserve signed net/difference representation and legitimate zero policy.",
    "Centralize repeatable boundary checks only where semantics match; do not replace distinct line allocation, fee authority and physical denomination policies with a universal parser."
  ],
  "required_tests": [
    "Per-command negative/zero/positive/extreme boundary matrix with permissions and atomic rejection.",
    "Preserve legitimate negative variance/net balance and zero-price/fee/comp; FIN016/018/cashcount regressions plus valid Deposit control."
  ],
